@using MUI.Catalog.Persistence
@using MUI.Discovery
@using MUI.Web.Accounts

@*
    What a claim actually grants, as two forms (spec §8.5, §11).

    Everything here is enrichment: the fields MSSP has no room for, and the owner's decision about
    their own connect screen. Nothing on this panel can reach a player count, a capability or a
    reachability history — the writable set is the field registry's own OwnerEnrichable flag, read
    below rather than listed here, and the endpoint refuses anything outside it out loud.

    An owner's answer is DECLARED. It is stored under its own field source beside whatever the
    crawler measured, shown with its age like every other fact, and it never replaces, hides or
    silences a measurement. The panel says so, because an operator typing into a box on a site whose
    whole claim is that its data is measured deserves to know which of the two they are producing.
*@

<section class="owner-panel">
    <h3>What only you can tell us about @Name</h3>

    <p class="faint">
        These are the things MSSP has no field for. They appear on your game's page as
        <em>owner-declared</em>, with the date you last confirmed them, beside what we measured —
        never instead of it. Nothing measured can be edited from here, by you or by us.
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/enrichment" class="enrichment">
        <AntiforgeryToken />

        @foreach (var definition in FieldRegistry.OwnerEnrichable)
        {
            <label>
                <span class="kicker">@definition.Name.ToLowerInvariant()</span>
                <input type="text"
                       name="@(OwnerWrites.FieldPrefix + definition.Name)"
                       value="@(Current(definition.Name)?.Value)"
                       maxlength="@OwnerEnrichment.MaxValueLength" />
                @if (Current(definition.Name) is { Value.Length: > 0 } row)
                {
                    <span class="faint">
                        declared @Relative.Format(Now - row.LastConfirmedAt) ago. Empty this box to
                        withdraw it — the record of what it said is kept either way.
                    </span>
                }
            </label>
        }

        <button type="submit">Save what you declared</button>
    </form>

    <h3>Your connect screen</h3>

    @if (Suppressed)
    {
        <p>
            We are not republishing it. The page says so plainly rather than leaving a hole, and the
            crawler goes on reading it — it is how we recognise your game if it moves.
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/connect-screen">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="suppress" value="false" />
            <button type="submit">Show it again</button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            We show it because your server sends it to every anonymous connection. If you would
            rather we did not, say so and we stop. We will not ask why.
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/connect-screen">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="suppress" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">Stop showing our connect screen</button>
        </form>
    }

    @*
        §8.5's WHO-format override. Placed after the enrichment form and visibly apart from it,
        because it is the one box on this panel that is not a fact about the game: everything above
        is published beside what we measured, and this is an instruction our parser acts on.
    *@
    <h3>How to read your WHO</h3>

    <p>
        We read your pre-login <code>WHO</code> structurally rather than by codebase, because Penn,
        MUX, Rhost and the TinyMUD family all let you rewrite the <code>DOING</code> header in
        softcode — and a header rewritten past our parser is why a busy game can show as never
        measured. If that is happening to @Name, tell us the line your table starts with.
    </p>

    <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/who-header" class="who-header">
        <AntiforgeryToken />
        <label>
            <span class="kicker">header line</span>
            <input type="text"
                   name="header"
                   value="@(Current(InternalFields.WhoHeader)?.Value)"
                   maxlength="@OwnerEnrichment.MaxValueLength" />
            <span class="faint">
                Any part of it is enough, and it is matched literally rather than as a pattern.
                Empty the box to withdraw it.
            </span>
        </label>
        <button type="submit">Save how to read it</button>
    </form>

    <p class="faint">
        <strong>This cannot change a number.</strong> It says where to start counting rows in text
        your server printed; the counting stays ours and the count stays measured. There is no box
        here for telling us how many people are online, and there will not be one — a site whose
        claim is that its data is measured cannot take the number from the person it is about.
    </p>

    @*
        §11's third route. The other two — the MSSP variable and the DNS TXT record — are answered by
        the game itself and need no account, which is why they are named here beside the button
        rather than hidden behind it: an owner who would rather not depend on us having their claim
        on file can say it in their own config and we will honour it on the next cycle.
    *@
    <h3>Being crawled</h3>

    @if (OptOut is { AllStopped: true })
    {
        <p>
            We have stopped. Nothing on @Name is dialled, and the page keeps everything measured
            before you asked — the empty hours name no cause, because <em>you asked us to stop</em>
            is our fact and not a measurement of your game.
        </p>

        @if (OptOut.WithdrawableHere)
        {
            <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/crawl">
                <AntiforgeryToken />
                <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="false" />
                <button type="submit">Start crawling us again</button>
            </form>
        }
        else
        {
            @* The game is still saying stop on every probe, and this side may not overrule it. *@
            <p class="faint">
                This one came from your own server rather than from here — @Routes. To be crawled
                again, stop publishing it; we will hear that on the next cycle.
            </p>
        }
    }
    else if (OptOut is { Partial: true })
    {
        @*
            Neither state, and shown as neither. Rounding this to "stopped" would tell an owner we
            had left them alone while we went on dialling the port that is still open.
        *@
        <p class="warn">
            We have stopped on @string.Join(", ", OptOut.Stopped) and are still dialling
            @string.Join(", ", OptOut.Dialling). That is usually a port added after the opt-out.
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/crawl">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">Stop on every address too</button>
        </form>
    }
    else
    {
        <p>
            We dial @Name on a schedule and read what any anonymous connection is shown. If you would
            rather we did not, say so and we stop — within one cycle, on every address we have for
            you, and we will not ask why.
        </p>
        <p class="faint">
            Nothing already measured is deleted: your page keeps its history and its URL, and one
            probe after you take this back starts it again. You can also say it without us, in your
            own config — <code>@OptOutVocabulary.MsspVariable</code> in MSSP, or a
            <code>@OptOutVocabulary.DnsLabel</code> TXT record — and we honour those whether or not
            anybody has ever claimed the game here.
        </p>
        <form method="post" action="/account/games/@GameId/crawl">
            <AntiforgeryToken />
            <input type="hidden" name="stop" value="true" />
            <button type="submit">Stop crawling us</button>
        </form>
    }
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public Guid GameId { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// What this game's owners have already declared, keyed by field.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Owner rows only, and passed in rather than fetched here so that one page load reads the
    /// catalogue once. A measurement is deliberately not offered back as a draft of itself: an
    /// editable box pre-filled with something we measured is an invitation to overwrite it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, GameField> Declared { get; set; } =
        new Dictionary<string, GameField>(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    [Parameter] public DateTimeOffset Now { get; set; } = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of this game's addresses we are honouring an opt-out for, or null where there is no
    /// register to ask — the demo fixture, which dials nothing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public OwnerOptOutState? OptOut { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The routes the standing rules came in by, for the case this side cannot undo.</summary>
    private string Routes => string.Join(", ", (OptOut?.Standing ?? [])
        .Select(rule => rule.Source switch
        {
            OptOutSource.Mssp => $"your MSSP report publishes {OptOutVocabulary.MsspVariable}",
            OptOutSource.DnsTxt => $"a {OptOutVocabulary.DnsLabel} TXT record asks us to stop",
            _ => "a request we recorded",
        })
        .Distinct());

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner's own decision, read from the field they set rather than from the rendered page.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The surfaces resolve suppression through the §5.1 precedence ladder, which is right for a
    /// reader and wrong for this form: the control has to reflect what <em>this owner</em> asked for
    /// and offer the opposite of it, and a ladder that ever put another source above theirs would
    /// otherwise show them a button that does nothing.
    /// </remarks>
    private bool Suppressed => string.Equals(
        Current(InternalFields.ConnectScreenSuppressed)?.Value, "true", StringComparison.Ordinal);

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner's row for a field, withdrawn or not.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A withdrawn field keeps an empty row (nothing is ever deleted), and the box beside it must
    /// not then claim it was "declared 5w ago" — an age on a value nobody can see is an age on
    /// nothing. Callers that render a declaration check the value as well as the row.
    /// </remarks>
    private GameField? Current(string field) =>
        Declared.TryGetValue(field, out var row) ? row : null;
}
